__d(function(e,a,t,o,s,r,n){s.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:7,chapter_title:"Getting to Know Plants",estimated_study_time_minutes:55,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Classify plants as herbs, shrubs, and trees based on their characteristics","Identify and describe the parts of a leaf: blade, petiole, midrib, veins, lamina","Distinguish between reticulate and parallel venation with examples","Distinguish between taproot and fibrous root systems with examples","Identify parts of a flower: sepals, petals, stamens, pistil","Understand the function of each part of a flower in reproduction","Describe the structure and function of the stem: support, transport of water","Connect plant structure to Karnataka's local flora and agriculture"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Basic awareness of different types of plants from everyday life","Chapter 1 knowledge: different plant parts are used as food","Ability to identify common plants like grass, rose, mango tree, neem"],introduction:{why_important:"Plants are the foundation of all life on Earth \u2014 they produce food, oxygen, and provide habitat. Understanding their structure helps us understand how they grow, reproduce, and function. This knowledge is essential for agriculture, gardening, and environmental conservation.",real_life_relevance:"When you see a grass plant, a rose bush, and a banyan tree \u2014 they look very different. Understanding why (herbs vs shrubs vs trees) helps you classify and understand the plant world. Farmers in Karnataka who understand root systems know which crops need what kind of soil preparation.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka is one of the most biodiverse states in India \u2014 the Western Ghats (part of which runs through Karnataka) are a UNESCO World Heritage biodiversity hotspot. Karnataka has sacred groves (devarakadus) with rare plant species. The state flower of Karnataka is the lotus (Nelumba nucifera). The state tree is the sandal tree (Santalum album), grown in Mysuru, Chamrajnagar, and Kodagu. Karnataka's Nagarhole and Bandipur national parks contain rare endemic plant species.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 1 introduced plant parts as food sources. This chapter goes deeper \u2014 examining the structure and function of each plant part, not just as food but as biological organs."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Herbs",role:"Small, soft-stemmed plants with a short lifespan \u2014 wheat, grass, mint, tulsi"},{name:"Shrubs",role:"Medium-sized plants with a hard but not very thick stem, branching near the base \u2014 rose, hibiscus, henna"},{name:"Trees",role:"Tall, long-lived plants with a thick, hard trunk \u2014 mango, neem, banyan, teak"},{name:"Creepers and Climbers",role:"Plants with weak stems that grow along the ground (creepers) or climb using support (climbers)"}],plot:["The chapter begins by asking: why do different plants look so different?","It classifies plants into herbs, shrubs, trees, creepers, and climbers based on stem characteristics.","It examines the stem in detail \u2014 its role in support, transport of water and nutrients, and food storage.","The leaf is studied: its parts (petiole, midrib, lamina/blade, veins) and the two types of venation (reticulate and parallel).","Root systems are explored: taproot (one main root with lateral branches \u2014 dicot plants) vs fibrous roots (many roots of similar size \u2014 monocot plants).","The chapter introduces the flower as the reproductive organ of a plant.","Parts of a flower are identified: calyx (sepals), corolla (petals), androecium (stamens \u2014 male), and gynoecium (pistil \u2014 female).","The functions of each floral part are explained.","The chapter connects plant structure to pollination and seed formation."],moral:"Plants are not just passive food \u2014 they are complex living organisms with specialised organs, each with specific functions that work together for survival and reproduction."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Types of Plants: Herbs, Shrubs, and Trees",simple_explanation:"Plants can be classified by the nature of their stem: soft-stemmed small plants (herbs), medium woody plants branching near base (shrubs), and tall plants with thick trunks (trees).",detailed_explanation:"Herbs: Plants with green, tender (non-woody), soft stems. They are usually small and short-lived. Examples: wheat, grass, mint, tulsi, mustard, rice, tomato plant. Shrubs: Plants with hard (slightly woody) stems that branch out near the base of the plant. They are medium-sized and live for several years. Examples: rose, hibiscus (China rose), lemon, henna (mehendi), coffee plant, tea plant. Trees: Tall plants with hard, woody trunks. The main stem (trunk) is thick, strong, and single. Branches grow higher up. They live for many years. Examples: mango, neem, banyan, teak, coconut, sandal. Creepers: Plants with very weak stems that grow (creep) along the ground. Examples: pumpkin, watermelon, bitter gourd (karela). Climbers: Plants with thin, weak stems that climb upward using support (tendrils, hooks, or twining). Examples: grapevine, money plant, pea plant, passion fruit.",real_life_example:"In your home: the tulsi (holy basil) plant in a small pot is a herb. The rose or hibiscus plant in the garden is a shrub. The big mango or coconut tree outside is a tree. The cucumber plant climbing on a fence is a climber.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's famous Sandal tree (Santalum album) \u2014 found in the forests of Mysuru, Chamrajnagar, and Kodagu \u2014 is a tree. The coffee plant (Coffea arabica/robusta) of Coorg (Kodagu) is a shrub. The rice plant widely grown in the Cauvery delta (Mandya, Mysuru, Shivamogga) is a herb. Bele (cucumber, bitter gourd) grown by Karnataka farmers are climbers.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse climbers and creepers. Creepers creep along the ground (pumpkin). Climbers climb upward using support (grapevine, money plant). Also, students sometimes call a banana plant a tree \u2014 it is actually a very tall herb because the 'trunk' is made of tightly packed leaf bases, not true woody tissue.",memory_tip:"H-S-T by size: Herb = tiny/short, Shrub = medium/branchy base, Tree = tall/thick trunk. Then add C-C: Creeper = crawls, Climber = climbs with help."},{concept_name:"The Stem",simple_explanation:"The stem supports the plant, conducts water and minerals upward, and transports food made in leaves downward.",detailed_explanation:"Functions of the stem: (1) Support: holds leaves, flowers, and fruits up toward sunlight. (2) Conduction: acts as a pipeline \u2014 xylem vessels carry water and minerals from roots to leaves; phloem vessels carry food (prepared by photosynthesis) from leaves to rest of plant. (3) Storage: some stems store food (potato tuber, sugarcane) or water (cactus). (4) Reproduction: some stems can grow into new plants (ginger, turmeric rhizomes; potato tubers). The stem has nodes (where leaves attach) and internodes (sections between nodes). Evidence for water transport: if you put a white flower stem in coloured water, the flower gradually turns the same colour \u2014 this shows water (and dissolved dye) moving upward through the stem.",real_life_example:"The classic experiment: take a white carnation or white flower and put its stem in red-coloured water. After a few hours, the petals turn slightly pink or red \u2014 proving that water moves upward through the stem.",karnataka_example:"Sugarcane (stems grown throughout Karnataka, especially in Belgaum/Belagavi and Hassan districts) has stems that store sugar \u2014 we eat the sweet stem juice. Ginger (Zingiber officinale), widely grown in Shivamogga and Uttara Kannada districts, is an underground stem (rhizome) that stores food.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think the stem only provides support. The stem's transport function (carrying water and food) is equally important. Also, students confuse the stem's upward transport (water/minerals via xylem) with downward transport (food via phloem) \u2014 both directions happen in the stem.",memory_tip:"Stem = Support + Send-water-up + Send-food-down. The stem is the building's plumbing and scaffolding combined."},{concept_name:"The Leaf: Parts and Venation",simple_explanation:"A leaf has a flat blade (lamina), a stalk (petiole), and a network of veins. Veins can be in a net pattern (reticulate) or parallel lines (parallel).",detailed_explanation:"Parts of a leaf: (1) Petiole: the stalk that attaches the leaf to the stem. (2) Lamina (blade): the flat, green part of the leaf where photosynthesis occurs. (3) Midrib: the thick central vein running down the middle of the leaf. (4) Veins: smaller branches from the midrib that carry water/nutrients and provide structural support to the leaf. (5) Leaf base: where the petiole attaches to the stem. Venation (the pattern of veins): Reticulate venation: veins branch out in an irregular net-like pattern. Examples: hibiscus, rose, mango, neem, bean, wheat. Parallel venation: veins run parallel to each other from base to tip (or across). Examples: grass, maize, banana, bamboo, sugarcane. Important: Reticulate venation \u2192 taproot system. Parallel venation \u2192 fibrous root system. This is a useful rule for plant classification.",real_life_example:"Hold a mango leaf (or any broad garden leaf) up to the light \u2014 you will see a network of veins branching from the midrib. Now hold a grass blade or banana leaf \u2014 you'll see veins running parallel side by side without branching.",karnataka_example:"The betel leaf (Piper betle), called 'Veelyada yele' in Kannada and traditionally used in Karnataka after meals, has clear reticulate venation \u2014 hold it up to sunlight and see the network. Banana leaves (used to serve food in traditional Karnataka meals) show clear parallel venation.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse reticulate and parallel venation on exams. Remember: reticulate = net (like a fishing net = reticulated web). Parallel = parallel railway tracks running side by side.",memory_tip:"RE-ticulate = RET = net (like internet = inter-NET). PARALLEL = P = Parallel lines. Mango leaf = net. Grass blade = parallel stripes."},{concept_name:"Root Systems: Taproot and Fibrous",simple_explanation:"Some plants have one thick main root with smaller side branches (taproot), while others have many thin roots of equal size with no main root (fibrous roots).",detailed_explanation:"Taproot system: A single, thick main root grows straight down into the soil. Many smaller lateral roots branch off from it. It penetrates deep soil to access water. Examples: carrot, radish, beetroot (the root is the food part itself), bean, pea, mango, neem. Plants with taproot systems also have reticulate leaf venation. Fibrous root system: Many thin roots of similar size grow from the base of the stem \u2014 there is no distinct main root. Roots are shallow but spread widely. Examples: grass, wheat, rice, maize, banana, onion. Plants with fibrous roots also have parallel leaf venation. Functions of roots: (1) Anchor the plant firmly in soil. (2) Absorb water and dissolved minerals from soil. (3) In some plants, store food (carrot, radish, sweet potato). (4) Some aerial roots provide additional support (banyan tree's prop roots).",real_life_example:"Pull a grass plant from the ground \u2014 you'll see many thin hair-like roots spreading in all directions (fibrous). Pull a carrot from the ground \u2014 you'll see the thick single root (taproot) with small branches. The taproot IS the carrot we eat.",karnataka_example:"Ragi (finger millet), the most important crop of Karnataka, has fibrous roots and parallel venation. This is why ragi is effective at preventing soil erosion \u2014 its fibrous roots bind soil tightly. Mango trees in Karnataka (found throughout the state) have taproots that penetrate deep soil, enabling the tree to survive dry Karnataka summers.",common_mistakes:"Students often get confused about which plant has which root type. Use the venation rule: Parallel venation = fibrous roots. Reticulate venation = taproot. This always works for this chapter. Also, carrot and radish are NOT the taproot \u2014 they ARE the taproot (modified, swollen taproot used as food storage).",memory_tip:"TAProot = one TAP that goes DEEP. FIBrous = many FIBres spreading wide. Parallel leaf = Fibrous root. Reticulate leaf = Taproot. PF-RT memory pair."},{concept_name:"Parts of a Flower",simple_explanation:"A flower has four whorls: sepals (outermost, green, protect the bud), petals (colourful, attract insects), stamens (male, produce pollen), and pistil (female, forms seeds).",detailed_explanation:"The four parts of a flower, from outermost to innermost: (1) Sepals (Calyx): The outermost whorl. Usually green and leaf-like. They enclose and protect the flower bud before it opens. After blooming, sepals are seen at the base of the flower. (2) Petals (Corolla): The second whorl. Usually brightly coloured and sometimes fragrant. Their function is to attract pollinators (insects, birds) for pollination. (3) Stamens (Androecium): The male reproductive part. Each stamen has a filament (stalk) and anther (tip that produces pollen). The anther produces pollen grains, which carry the male genetic material. (4) Pistil / Carpel (Gynoecium): The female reproductive part, in the centre. Has three parts: stigma (sticky top that receives pollen), style (tube connecting stigma to ovary), ovary (contains ovules which become seeds after fertilisation). A flower with all four whorls is called a complete flower.",real_life_example:"Take a hibiscus (china rose/dasavala) flower \u2014 you can clearly see the green sepals at the base, the large colourful petals, the long stamen tube with anthers, and the stigma protruding from the top. Hibiscus is the classic teaching flower for this chapter.",karnataka_example:"The lotus (Nelumba nucifera), Karnataka's state flower, has distinct sepals, petals, many stamens, and a unique flat-topped receptacle containing multiple pistils. Champak flowers (Magnolia champaca) used in Karnataka temples have clearly visible stamens and pistils. Jasmine (mallige) \u2014 Karnataka's state flower \u2014 provides fragrant petals used in hair flowers and is grown in districts like Dharwad and Udupi.",common_mistakes:"Students confuse stamens and pistil (male and female). Remember: STAMEN = MALE (S and M are linked \u2014 Stamen, Male). PISTIL = FEMALE (P for Pistil, contains the ovary). Also, students sometimes count petals and call the whole flower the 'stamen' \u2014 stamen is only the pollen-producing male part.",memory_tip:"Flower from outside to centre: S-P-S-P = Sepal, Petal, Stamen, Pistil. OR: Some Pretty Students Pass \u2014 Sepal, Petal, Stamen, Pistil. Male = Stamen (anther produces pollen). Female = Pistil (ovary makes seeds)."}],key_vocabulary:[{term:"Herb",definition:"A small plant with a soft, green (non-woody) stem. Usually short-lived. Examples: grass, mint, wheat."},{term:"Shrub",definition:"A medium-sized plant with a slightly woody stem that branches near the base. Examples: rose, hibiscus, henna."},{term:"Tree",definition:"A tall, long-lived plant with a thick, single woody trunk. Examples: mango, neem, banyan."},{term:"Creeper",definition:"A plant with a very weak stem that grows along the ground. Examples: pumpkin, watermelon."},{term:"Climber",definition:"A plant with a weak stem that uses support (tendrils, hooks) to grow upward. Examples: grapevine, pea plant."},{term:"Petiole",definition:"The stalk of a leaf that connects the leaf blade (lamina) to the stem."},{term:"Lamina",definition:"The flat, broad blade part of a leaf where photosynthesis takes place."},{term:"Venation",definition:"The pattern of veins in a leaf. Can be reticulate (net-like) or parallel."},{term:"Reticulate venation",definition:"A net-like branching pattern of veins. Seen in mango, rose, hibiscus, neem. Associated with taproot."},{term:"Parallel venation",definition:"Veins running parallel to each other. Seen in grass, maize, banana, rice. Associated with fibrous roots."},{term:"Taproot",definition:"A root system with one thick main root going deep into soil, with smaller lateral branches. Examples: carrot, mango, bean."},{term:"Fibrous roots",definition:"A root system with many thin roots of similar size, no main root. Examples: grass, wheat, rice, onion."},{term:"Sepal",definition:"The outermost, usually green whorl of a flower that protects the bud before it opens."},{term:"Petal",definition:"The colourful whorl of a flower that attracts pollinators."},{term:"Stamen",definition:"The male reproductive part of a flower. Has anther (produces pollen) and filament (stalk)."},{term:"Pistil",definition:"The female reproductive part of a flower. Has stigma, style, and ovary (contains ovules/future seeds)."}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Types of Plants",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Classify: banana plant, rose plant, mango tree, money plant.",a:"Banana \u2014 herb (soft stem base, not woody). Rose \u2014 shrub (branches near base, slightly woody). Mango \u2014 tree (tall, thick woody trunk). Money plant \u2014 climber (uses support to grow upward)."},{type:"Application",q:"Why is the banana plant considered a herb despite being very tall?",a:"The banana plant's 'trunk' is actually made of tightly packed leaf bases (not woody tissue). A true tree has hard, woody cells in its trunk. The banana's trunk is soft and non-woody, making it a herb by botanical definition \u2014 the world's tallest herb."}]},{after_concept:"Root Systems",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"A plant has parallel venation in its leaves. What type of root system does it have?",a:"Fibrous root system. There is a reliable correlation: parallel venation = fibrous roots. Examples: grass, rice, maize all have parallel venation and fibrous roots."}]},{after_concept:"Parts of a Flower",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name the male and female parts of a flower.",a:"Male part = Stamen (with anther that produces pollen). Female part = Pistil/Carpel (with stigma, style, and ovary containing ovules)."}]}],key_takeaways:["Plants are classified as herbs (soft stem), shrubs (woody branching near base), trees (thick trunk), creepers (along ground), or climbers (with support).","Banana plant is the world's tallest herb \u2014 its 'trunk' is made of leaf bases, not wood.","The stem supports the plant and conducts water (up via xylem) and food (down via phloem).","Leaf parts: petiole, lamina, midrib, veins.","Reticulate venation (net pattern) = taproot system. Parallel venation = fibrous root system.","Taproot: one thick main root (carrot, bean, mango). Fibrous roots: many thin equal roots (grass, rice, wheat).","Flower parts from outside in: Sepals \u2192 Petals \u2192 Stamens \u2192 Pistil.","Stamen = male (anther produces pollen). Pistil = female (ovary makes seeds).","Karnataka: Sandal tree (state tree), lotus (state flower), ragi has fibrous roots, coffee plant is a shrub."],mind_map:{root:"Getting to Know Plants",branches:[{branch:"Types of Plants",sub:["Herb \u2014 soft stem, small (wheat, tulsi)","Shrub \u2014 woody, branching near base (rose, hibiscus)","Tree \u2014 tall, thick trunk (mango, neem)","Creeper \u2014 along ground (pumpkin)","Climber \u2014 uses support (grapevine, pea)"]},{branch:"Leaf",sub:["Parts: petiole, lamina, midrib, veins","Reticulate venation \u2014 net pattern (mango, rose) \u2192 taproot","Parallel venation \u2014 parallel lines (grass, rice) \u2192 fibrous roots"]},{branch:"Roots",sub:["Taproot \u2014 one thick main root (carrot, radish, bean)","Fibrous roots \u2014 many thin roots (grass, wheat, ragi)","Functions: anchor, absorb water, store food"]},{branch:"Stem",sub:["Support","Water transport upward (xylem)","Food transport downward (phloem)","Storage in some (potato, sugarcane, ginger)"]},{branch:"Flower",sub:["Sepals \u2014 protect bud (outermost)","Petals \u2014 attract pollinators (colourful)","Stamen \u2014 male (anther produces pollen)","Pistil \u2014 female (ovary \u2192 seeds)"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["State tree: Sandal (Mysuru, Kodagu)","State flower: Lotus","Ragi \u2014 fibrous roots, parallel venation","Coffee shrub (Coorg)","Sugarcane stem \u2014 food storage"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 7 classifies plants and studies their parts. Plant types: herbs (soft stem \u2014 wheat, tulsi, rice), shrubs (hard stem branching near base \u2014 rose, hibiscus, coffee), trees (thick trunk \u2014 mango, neem, teak), creepers (along ground \u2014 pumpkin), climbers (use support \u2014 grapevine). Banana = tallest herb (soft trunk). Stem functions: support, water transport up (xylem), food transport down (phloem), storage (potato, sugarcane, ginger). Leaf parts: petiole (stalk), lamina (flat blade), midrib (central vein), veins. Venation: reticulate = net pattern (mango, rose, bean) \u2192 associated with taproot; parallel = parallel lines (grass, rice, banana) \u2192 associated with fibrous roots. Roots: taproot = one thick main root with laterals (carrot, radish, bean, mango); fibrous = many equal thin roots, no main root (grass, wheat, rice, ragi). Flower parts (outside to inside): sepals (protect bud, usually green), petals (colourful, attract pollinators), stamens (male \u2014 anther produces pollen + filament), pistil (female \u2014 stigma + style + ovary containing ovules). Karnataka: sandal tree (state tree), lotus (state flower), ragi (fibrous roots, parallel venation), coffee shrub in Coorg.",must_remember_facts:["Herb = soft stem. Shrub = woody, branches near base. Tree = thick woody trunk.","Banana plant = herb (tallest herb in the world \u2014 trunk is leaf bases, not wood).","Reticulate venation (net) \u2192 Taproot. Parallel venation (lines) \u2192 Fibrous roots.","Taproot examples: carrot, radish, bean, mango. Fibrous root examples: grass, wheat, rice, ragi.","Stem functions: support + water up (xylem) + food down (phloem) + storage.","Flower parts outside to inside: Sepal \u2192 Petal \u2192 Stamen \u2192 Pistil.","Stamen = MALE (anther produces pollen). Pistil = FEMALE (ovary contains ovules \u2192 seeds).","Karnataka: Sandal = state tree; Lotus = state flower; Coffee = shrub in Coorg; Ragi = fibrous roots."]}},2531,[]);